all the time video games were built on  the backs of larger-than-life heroes  we've mowed our way through demons on  mars banish villains from a dozen  different high rules and toppled  dangerous ideologies in underwater  dystopias for most of the mediums  history games have granted us superhuman  powers and expansive Arsenal's of  weaponry but in 2012 four people began  work on a game that would go against  that current they had worked on triple-a  titles such as Bioshock 2 and BioShock  Infinite and recognized the potential  for a different kind of game so they  formed the fulbright company and began  work on their first release one that  took the environmental storytelling  aspects of the Bioshock series and  stripped away the rest weapons  superpowers over-the-top characters and  all in gone home players would explore a  mysterious house in the 1990s finding  clues to the whereabouts of their  missing family members  gaining insight through the things they  left behind five years later gone home  is lauded for the way it went against  the grain it spawned a wave of games in  its wake those that place us in the  shoes of ordinary people in relatable  situations and also prioritizing  narrative power over frantic action  what's more it told the coming-of-age  tale of gay characters diving headfirst  into topics that games as a medium have  often avoided since their inception now  four years after gone homes release we  traveled to Portland Oregon to discuss  the game's impact with those who made it  and those who have since joined the team  to relive how gone home sparked a new  genre challenge the definition of a  videogame and influenced their second  game Tacoma in 2017  but with a game like we make really what  you need is you know that kind of like  word of mouth thing for people to say I  played this game and now you have to  play it the biggest compliment you can  get and I think they were multiple of  these four gone home we're like reviews  that say if yo